00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.640
Firebase is going to be a
really good option for us

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.550
because of its excellent
A/B testing capabilities

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.270
and also because it helps
us set up our OAuth flow.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.300
Over 3 million developers
from startups to enterprises

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.050
have been using Firebase to
engage billions of users.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:07.940
In this demo,
we're going to show

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.310
you additional
benefits of this stack,

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.910
like the downstream integration
with Cloud Functions, Cloud

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:20.040
Firestore, BigQuery, Cloud
Run, and other services.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.950
Now, to reach our
addressable market,

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.500
our app needs to be accessible
by everyone in our community.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.250
And that means we need
to support languages

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.530
from around the world.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:35.650
The Google Cloud Translate
API is perfect for this.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.870
Cloud Functions for
Firebase can, in fact,

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:42.660
be used to trigger
the Translate app API.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.900
Here's a simple function
that we've written.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.350
Let's test it and try
translating the phrase

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:53.570
from English to my
favorite language, Hindi.

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.710
Here is the output in Hindi.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:04:04.180
[SPEAKING HINDI] And with
a few more lines of code,

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:07.380
we can support not just Hindi
but over a hundred world

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:08.280
languages.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.520
Now, to really test
product market fit,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.180
we're going to add an in-app
feedback form to the community

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.690
app, and that's going to help
us see what's resonating with

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.670
our users and what isn't.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.860
A common challenge
here is making

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.950
sense of the unstructured,
free-text feedback data

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.960
that we're going to
hear from our users.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.770
This, again, is where Google
Cloud's integrated stack

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.090
comes in.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.880
As you can see, this
stack integrates

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:41.860
Firebase with BigQuery,
Vertex AI, and other services

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:43.870
through Cloud Firestore.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:48.580
So what you get here is
Database, Compute, Analytics,

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.850
and AI and ML services
integrated together

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:56.020
for a faster application
development experience.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.240
Cloud Firestore is
an excellent option

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.040
for mobile and web development.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.980
It's a NoSQL database
with built-in integrations

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.040
to Firebase and Flutter
and to Google Cloud.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.530
And because it's
serverless, we never

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:11.260
have to worry about
maintaining it or scaling it.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.170
In our app, the incoming
customer feedback

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:17.410
is free-text data, and we're
storing it in Firestore.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:21.710
So let's check this out in
Firestore for the comments.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:25.280
Here, we see that our users
are saying, "How can I

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:26.870
find organizations near me?"

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.660
Or "This app is amazing."

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:33.590
Well, it looks like some of
our users are liking the app.

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:37.640
Firestore has a really great
integration with BigQuery.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.850
It easily exports
the data to BigQuery.

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:45.830
BigQuery is an industry-leading,
fully managed, serverless cloud

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:49.100
data warehouse that can scale
to hundreds of petabytes.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:52.610
BigQuery customers
analyze over 110 terabytes

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:56.630
of data per second, so we're
in very good hands here.

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:59.460
Let's check out how this works.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:04.280
We've installed the Firestore
to BigQuery extension.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:07.970
Here, you can see the
customer feedback.

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:11.570
"Love the new features," as
well as "How do I upgrade

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.820
my app to unlock donations?"

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.820
This is all in Cloud Store.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.280
These same comments
can also now be

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.120
seen in BigQuery, thanks to the
extension we just installed.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.800
Super straightforward,
easy, yet very powerful,

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:29.780
because with our
data and BigQuery,

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:34.100
we can uncover deep insights
quickly using Vertex AI.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:37.580
We're going to use
Vertex AI to categorize

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.400
each of our user inputs into
one of three categories.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.030
It's either a bug
report, or it's feedback,

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.720
or it's a pricing issue.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.710
Vertex AI is Google Cloud's
unified artificial intelligence

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:51.950
platform.

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:54.670
It provides building blocks
for various machine learning

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:58.840
applications, like Vision,
Video, and many more.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:02.330
Vertex AI offers core
services for data science,

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:07.640
machine learning, and ML ops
in a single unified platform.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:11.650
You can also go deeper into
custom model development.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:14.560
Overall, in comparison
to other platforms,

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.910
training a model with Vertex
AI requires 80% less code.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:21.160
And most importantly,
you don't need

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:26.200
to be an ML expert or a
PhD to leverage Vertex AI.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:30.070
Let's see this in action
and classify our user input

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:31.870
into those three categories.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:36.220
We've trained a model to detect
different types of feedback.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:39.160
Check out our model
here in the Models tab.

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.880
We click on the model
name, and it takes us

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:46.440
to the confusion matrix.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:47.910
That's shown in blue.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:54.730
This matrix shows us how
often the model has classified

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:57.320
each label correctly.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.210
Over here, we have
the Test and Deploy

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:04.910
tab that allows us to deploy
our model to a REST endpoint.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:10.610
From there, we can easily call
it from a web or mobile app.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.820
Plus, it lets us make
sample predictions

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.930
right here from within the UI.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.930
Let's try a sample now.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.120
Here's a text--
"the loading times

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:26.110
are so slow now on my Pixel 4."

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:28.660
In fact, the model
correctly identified

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:32.600
that this is a bug report.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.880
Shall we try another one?

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.960
This text says, "thank
you for this release

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:40.690
the new features are great."

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:45.940
And we can see that the model
predicted this to be feedback

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:48.250
and does that with
pretty high confidence.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:54.550
Now, let's say we want to use
this model from a mobile or web

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:55.600
app.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.470
Right here in Vertex AI,
we can see the Python code

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:01.660
that we would need.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.810
This code can be used to
call this cloud-hosted model

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:07.220
from our app.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:12.180
There's also client libraries
in other languages available.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.250
But we actually want
to use this model

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.970
to make predictions on our text
data that's stored in BigQuery.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:23.070
So how can we call Python
functions on BigQuery data?

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:27.630
Well, to do that, we will use
BigQuery remote functions.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:31.380
This is a brand-new
capability in BigQuery.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.920
Here, you see our customer
input data in BigQuery

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:38.200
without any categories.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:40.600
Let's add a
classification column

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.195
to show the categories
assigned by Vertex AI.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.560
We're going to use a Cloud
Function to call the Vertex

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:53.670
model we just built to
classify each piece of input

00:09:53.670 --> 00:10:00.440
as a bug report, feedback, or
pricing issue within BigQuery.

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:03.380
You can use any language
that Cloud Function supports,

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.750
but today, as you can
see, we're using Python.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.640
Next, we'll call this function
using SQL within BigQuery.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.560
Let's run the query.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:21.210
And here, you can see the
user input data is categorized

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:24.450
right back here in BigQuery.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:27.750
BigQuery remote functions
are not only easy to use

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:30.780
but also very
powerful because they

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:35.310
combine BigQuery with any
Google Cloud product or API.

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:37.620
That was a great
example of automation,

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:41.520
but there's much more you can
do with Google Cloud Services.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.910
Let's take this further and
automate the appropriate action

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:47.880
for each category
of our user input.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:52.260
We want bug reports filed
on GitHub as issues.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.010
Guess what?

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:54.750
That's easy.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:58.170
We can use Cloud Run and its
newly launched serverless

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:00.810
jobs capability that
lets us run batch

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:06.410
jobs on demand, on a schedule,
or via an event trigger.

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:09.650
We've built a Cloud Run job
to extract the bug reports

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:13.160
from our data and create
GitHub issues for our team

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:15.840
to triage and fix.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.180
I can schedule my job to
execute whenever I want it to.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:23.360
I've been running it nightly,
but let's trigger the job

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:26.790
manually to see it run now.

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:29.280
While it's running,
I'll mention that this

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:31.080
isn't the only job we have.

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:32.820
We love Cloud Run jobs.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:36.330
So we also have jobs to
move customer service issues

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:40.170
to the customer service
ticket tracker and a job

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:43.200
to create an email summary
of each day's feedback

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.490
for our executives.

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:49.330
And now our job has
finished executing.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:53.140
And you can see that there's
a new issue tagged Mobile Bug

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:57.050
Report in GitHub,
just as it should be.

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:00.040
This has the comments that a
user put into our mobile app

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:01.790
earlier.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:05.760
Cloud Run jobs can run
multiple tasks in parallel.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.810
So we can scale
our app as we grow.

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:12.080
I encourage you to try out
Firestore, Cloud Functions,

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:15.440
Cloud Run jobs,
BigQuery, and Vertex AI

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:19.550
to accelerate the time to market
of your latest innovations.

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:23.060
Once you've found product market
fit, a new set of challenges

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:27.650
will await you as your app
grows to millions of users.

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:31.520
These day-two or day-three
challenges include scalability,

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:36.740
observability, and software
supply chain security.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:39.700
Fortunately, we have used
serverless technology

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:41.620
throughout our app so far.

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:44.980
BigQuery, Cloud
Functions, and Cloud Run

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:47.720
are fully managed
serverless capabilities.

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:49.690
So as we get more
users, they're going

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:51.940
to scale up automatically.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:53.980
But let's look at a more
difficult scalability

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:56.910
challenge.

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:01.020
As our app grew, so did the
volunteers and opportunities,

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:05.340
and we decided to add a map view
to our app to show volunteers

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:06.930
what opportunities are nearby.

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:09.630
Google Maps platform
is a popular API

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:12.720
amongst developers because
of its scale, coverage,

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:14.700
and familiarity.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:18.510
We stored our volunteer and
opportunity location data

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:22.650
in a Postgres database because
Postgres has great location

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:25.300
data support out of the box.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:28.140
But as it turned
out, our growth was

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:32.010
much faster than the Postgres
database could handle.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:35.010
These red pins here on
the map show how strong

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:37.440
the interest has been.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.960
Now our app has
started slowing down,

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.900
and we're getting complaints
about volunteer numbers

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:46.500
not showing up right, as well
as people signing up for already

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:48.540
filled opportunities.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.860
What are we going to do?

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:53.370
Well, we could
optimize our database,

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:56.070
but that would require having
the right expertise in house

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:59.420
or the time to hire for it.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:02.090
We could shrink the scope of
volunteers and opportunities

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:04.220
to reduce the load
on our database,

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:05.990
but that's not
really a good option

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:09.510
because it would limit
the growth of our app.

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:12.270
Thankfully, we don't
need to do either.

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:15.480
AlloyDB is a new and
powerful relational database

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:19.710
from Google Cloud that's fully
compatible with PostgreSQL.

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:23.190
With AlloyDB, we can easily
build apps and scale them

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:26.910
to millions of users, all while
using existing open source

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:28.620
Postgres skills.

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:31.680
And because AlloyDB is
fully Postgres compatible,

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:34.830
we can switch our Postgres
instance to AlloyDB

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:37.410
without making any
changes to our application

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:40.170
code or our queries.

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:43.020
Most importantly here, the
accelerated performance

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:46.410
of AlloyDB means that we can
continue querying our volunteer

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:50.495
data in real time without
slowing down the app.

00:14:50.495 --> 00:14:50.995
Success.

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:57.510
Our next day-two challenge
is observability,

00:14:57.510 --> 00:14:59.880
which involves
troubleshooting and isolating

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:03.240
root causes of potential
service disruptions.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:06.310
Google Cloud has
us covered here.

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:10.750
First, our compute options,
BigQuery and Firebase,

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:13.240
have in-context
observability data

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:15.880
right within the Cloud Console.

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:20.140
This one unified view across
logs, events, metrics,

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:23.680
and service-level objectives
requires zero setup

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:25.810
to help quickly
connect the dots and

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:28.650
root-cause day-two problems.

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:31.560
In addition to this,
day-two ops also

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:35.520
requires cohesive monitoring,
visualization, and diagnostics

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.800
at the network level.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:39.960
That's why today, I'm
thrilled to announce

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:43.830
the launch of Network
Analyzer, a new tool that's

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:47.490
part of the Network Intelligence
Center in Google Cloud.

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:51.720
Network Analyzer proactively
analyzes configurations,

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:56.280
firewall rules, routes, IP
addresses, and network capacity

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:59.400
utilization to detect
potential problems

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:01.950
and make recommendations
that help you minimize

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:03.360
mean time to resolution.

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:08.650
Last but not least,
we developers

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:13.120
increasingly need to plan for
potential security attacks.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.250
All of these can
happen at any time.

00:16:15.250 --> 00:16:18.580
They are particularly damaging
to an at-scale production

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:20.870
system.

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:22.610
Recent software
supply chain attacks,

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:25.760
like SolarWinds,
Mimecast, and Codecov,

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:29.360
show the importance of securing
each link in our supply chain,

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:32.600
right from source
through to deploy.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:35.390
Software supply chain
security is natively

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:39.750
built into Google Cloud's
developer tool chain.

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:42.290
This can be a great
benefit for developers,

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:44.840
especially if you're in
a regulated industry,

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:47.900
such as fintech or retail.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:50.550
Cloud Build, our
service for building,

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:55.580
testing, and deploying code to
VMs, serverless, Kubernetes,

00:16:55.580 --> 00:17:01.370
or Firebase, now offers SLSA
level 2 compliance by default.

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:05.450
SLSA is an emerging standard
that encapsulates industry

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:09.349
best practices for software
supply chain integrity.

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:12.660
Cloud Build implements
these practices by default,

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:17.290
so you and I don't have
to be security experts.

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:21.640
Cloud Build also provides
verifiable build provenance.

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:25.630
This provenance helps you trace
a binary to its source code

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:28.000
to prevent tampering
and to prove

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:30.250
that the code you're
running is the code that you

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:32.260
think you're running.

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:35.980
You can see here an attestation.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:38.950
This is metadata
about the build.

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:42.010
It's labeled
built-by-cloud-build.

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:44.320
This metadata indicates
that the build

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:46.690
is legitimate
because it's signed

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:49.420
by Cloud Build, the service.

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:53.950
Cloud Build is the first service
to offer a zero-configuration,

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:57.730
out-of-the-box experience
for build provenance.

00:17:57.730 --> 00:17:59.680
Stitching together
this type of experience

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:03.280
is not straightforward
in competing products.

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:07.060
Google Cloud also offers Binary
Authorization integration,

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:09.910
which ensures that only
builds verified by Cloud Build

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:12.220
are deployed to production.

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:15.380
Here, you can see us
trying to deploy an image.

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:19.720
This image is not verified
or signed by Cloud Build.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:22.150
And so, with Binary
Authorization,

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:23.710
this deployment step fails.

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:28.760
And when we try
deploying an image that

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:33.730
is signed by Cloud Build, it
goes through to deployment.

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:40.600
In summary, what we've shown you
today showcases our integrated

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:45.190
end-to-end stack and how it can
help you get started quickly

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:48.490
to achieve accelerated
time to market on day one

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:52.420
and also to proactively solve
your pressing day-two and

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:55.930
day-three issues, like
scalability, observability,

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:58.680
and software supply
chain security.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:02.190
Before I go, I want to talk
about one more innovation that

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:04.670
is hot off the presses.

00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:07.760
It allows you to easily
develop interactive experiences

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:10.550
for your customers
and prospects.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:14.870
We're excited to announce
Immersive Stream for XR.

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:18.260
This service renders
extended reality experiences

00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:22.650
using Google Cloud's
GPUs, and it streams them

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:25.950
to millions of mobile
devices around the world.

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:29.400
By using Immersive
Stream for XR,

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:32.970
you can engage your users in
an immersive, interactive,

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:35.910
photorealistic experience
without them ever

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:37.950
having to download an app.

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:41.730
And best of all, you'll save
time on development and testing

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:44.730
because you can simply
create content once,

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:46.980
knowing that it will run
on any mobile device,

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:50.870
regardless of model,
year, or operating system.

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:54.500
Immersive Stream for XR
powers our interactive flyover

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:59.460
experience for Maps that Sundar
announced during his keynote.

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:01.950
And it's also powering a
next-generation car-buying

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:06.040
experience from Ford,
as well as my favorite,

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:08.860
an augmented reality
kitchen visualizer

00:20:08.860 --> 00:20:13.130
by Lowe's, and a virtual
shopping assistant by KDDI.

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:19.600
To get started, visit this URL
including interactive demos

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:20.830
from our partners.

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:22.390
We can't wait to
see what you create

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:25.000
with this technology
and the end-to-end stack

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:27.040
from Google Cloud.

